<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nasir Jung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1712">1712</a>–<a href="/years/1750">1750</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nasir Jung was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Asaf Jah I</a> who succeeded his father as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a> in 1748. Backed by the British, he fought the claims of his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-jung">Muzaffar Jung</a> and the French in the Carnatic. He was killed in December 1750 by one of his own Pathan allies during a battle with the French.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nasir-jung.jpg" alt="Nasir Jung" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hyderabad, India (made) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nasir_Jung,_Nizam_of_Hyderabad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mir Ahmad Ali Khan Siddiqi Bayafandi was born on 26 February 1712, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Asaf Jah I</a> and his wife Saeed-un-nisa Begum. The Mughal emperor Muhammad Shah gave him the title Nasir Jung, and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-bahadur">Ahmad Shah Bahadur</a> appointed him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subedar">Subedar</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> with the title Nasir-ud-Daula. He acted as his father's deputy while Asaf Jah was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> from 1737 to 1741. In 1741 he tried to seize power for himself but was defeated by his father at the Eid Gah Maidan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Aurangabad</a> on 23 July 1741. He later served as Subedar of Aurangabad in 1745 and 1746.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After his father's death Nasir Jung took the throne at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhanpur">Burhanpur</a> on 2 June 1748 as the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-jung">Muzaffar Jung</a>, the old Nizam's grandson through a daughter, disputed the succession, and Hussain Dost Khan, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanda-sahib">Chanda Sahib</a>, joined Muzaffar Jung's cause in the hope of becoming Nawab of the Carnatic in place of Anwar-ud-din Muhammad Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a>.</p><p>The quarrel drew in the rival British and French trading companies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-francois-dupleix">Joseph François Dupleix</a>, governor-general of French India, saw a chance to extend French influence; since the two nations were at peace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, both sides worked through alliances and local fighting, which became the Second Carnatic War. The French backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanda-sahib">Chanda Sahib</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-jung">Muzaffar Jung</a>, while the British East India Company supported Nasir Jung and Muhammad Ali Khan Walajah, whose father Anwar-ud-din had been killed at the Battle of Ambur in 1749. By 1750 the French and their allies had won a series of successes in both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and the Carnatic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During one of those French successes, on 16 December 1750, Nasir Jung was killed at Sarasangupettai, also called Dupleix-Fathabad, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gingee">Gingee</a>, by the Pathan Himmat Khan. Muzaffar Jung then took the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>. Nasir Jung was buried beside his father at Khuldabad, in the shrine of Burhan ud-Din Gharib. The war went on: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a>'s capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a> in 1751 began a run of British victories, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a> of 1754-55 recognised Muhammad Ali Khan Walajah as Nawab of the Carnatic, while Dupleix was recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nasir Jung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gingee">Gingee</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.25,79.42&amp;year=1736" aria-label="Gingee on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Nasir Jung"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4121 3960h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4121 3960h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=12.25,79.42&amp;year=1736">What was here in 1736? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
